1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,880
 Hey everyone, Ryan here, and welcome back to our series on prosthodontics.

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:09,360
 Like all my videos, I'm going to be focusing only on the highest deal of things you need

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,240
 to know for the exam.

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:16,440
 And I know we already talked about the basics of occlusion in our second video of this series,

5
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:22,600
 but here we're going to look more into the occlusion for complete dentures, and understanding

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,520
 a patient's occlusion is critical to making a proper denture.

7
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:33,220
 So if you haven't watched the first two videos in this series, I highly recommend you check

8
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:38,280
 those out first, because I'll be using a lot of the terms in this video that I introduced

9
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:39,780
 in those.

10
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:45,480
 So there are basically three really important measurements to know for complete dentures.

11
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:51,520
 That is VDO, VDR, and inter occlusal rest space.

12
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:56,840
 So the first one we'll talk about is vertical dimension of rest, which is VDR, and it's

13
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,800
 shown in yellow in this image.

14
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:08,960
 So the vertical dimension of rest is the distance measured between the nose and chin at rest.

15
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:14,600
 Now sometimes it's measured using other points, but the most stable points of interest would

16
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,560
 be the tip of the nose and the tip of the chin.

17
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:22,240
 So those are typically used for measuring the distance.

18
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:27,560
 It's not only the distance between the nose and the chin, but it's at rest, and specifically

19
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:32,640
 where elevator and depressor muscles are in a state of equilibrium.

20
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:39,040
 This is sometimes called physiologic rest position, or PRP for short.

21
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:47,120
 So if you're just relaxing your jaw, if you swallow, and maybe slightly open your mouth,

22
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:51,640
 that's sort of the most relaxed position for you, that would be your vertical dimension

23
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:52,640
 of rest.

24
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:58,820
 And so this is for a dentalist patients, this is for dentate patients, everyone has a vertical

25
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,840
 dimension of rest.

26
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:07,080
 And in this position there is usually, if you have teeth, 3 millimeters of space between

27
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:13,920
 the upper and lower premolars, and that number will become important a little bit later.

28
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:20,600
 So let's just say for this patient, when he's at rest, distance between tip of chin and tip

29
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:27,040
 of the nose is, let's say, 10 centimeters.

30
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:31,880
 Alright so VDO stands for vertical dimension of occlusion.

31
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:36,480
 And that's the distance, again, between the nose and the chin, but when the patient is

32
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:41,000
 biting together, and that's where the occlusion comes in.

33
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,440
 And that's going to be represented with the white arrow in this image.

34
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:51,620
 So it indicates the superior-inferior relationship of the maxilla and the mandible when the teeth

35
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:55,080
 are occluded in maximum intracuspation.

36
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:01,400
 Remember MI was one of those key occlusal positions that we talked about in our very

37
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,200
 first video.

38
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:10,480
 So you can imagine how the vertical dimension of occlusion would be very different if, say,

39
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:17,280
 chin, if the patient has teeth versus if they're missing posterior teeth, or don't have any

40
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,880
 teeth at all.

41
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:24,360
 So let's just say for this patient, it's 9 and a half centimeters.

42
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:29,920
 And you can imagine that when this patient bites together, the tip of his chin gets up

43
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:32,080
 to this line here.

44
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:37,120
 So this is showing his vertical dimension of rest, and you can imagine again, if he was

45
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,840
 biting together, his chin would get maybe up to this level.

46
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:45,200
 So that would mean the vertical dimension of occlusion, again, is maybe let's say half

47
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:51,960
 a centimeter shorter for this patient.

48
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,840
 And so that leaves us with the final measurement.

49
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:59,760
 That's an intraclusal space, and that's the difference between these two distances, that

50
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:05,480
 is VDR and VDO, and it's ideally 2 to 4 millimeters.

51
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,000
 And that's where that 3 millimeters comes from.

52
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,360
 That was usually 3 millimeters of distance between the upper and lower premolars when

53
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:16,280
 a patient's at rest.

54
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:22,800
 And so we can get this equation that vertical dimension of rest is equal to the vertical

55
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:29,440
 dimension of occlusion when the patient's biting down, plus about 3 millimeters of intraclusal

56
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:34,840
 rest space, which would be the distance between these two positions.

57
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:40,840
 And this is an ideal scenario, so it varies depending on the type of patient you're working

58
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:41,840
 with.

59
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:48,120
 So in our example, we said vertical dimension of rest would be 10 centimeters, and the vertical

60
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:52,080
 dimension of occlusion would be 9.5 centimeters.

61
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:59,360
 So the distance between those two, or the difference rather between those two, would be 0.5 centimeters

62
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:02,160
 or 5 millimeters.

63
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:06,660
 So you may be thinking, well, okay, that makes sense, but what if the distance between the

64
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:14,080
 two is too large or too short and is way far away from this 3 millimeter mark?

65
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:17,680
 So we'll talk about that right now.

66
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:25,040
 So let's say we have excessive VDO, that would be where the intraclusal space is less than

67
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:32,920
 2 millimeters, or maybe even the VDO is bigger than the vertical dimension of rest.

68
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:40,040
 So this would cause a whole bunch of symptoms, excessive display of mandibular teeth, muscles

69
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:45,920
 of mastication being fatigued, and that's a really important one to know for the exam.

70
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:50,760
 Clicking of the posterior teeth when speaking, strained appearance of the lips as you can

71
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:57,520
 see in this image, the patient would be unable to wear the dentures due to discomfort, excessive

72
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:00,980
 trauma to supporting tissues, and gagging.

73
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:08,200
 So this is a whole host of issues if you have an excessive vertical dimension of occlusion.

74
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:14,840
 So this would be if you made a set of dentures for a previously a dentulous patient, and the

75
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:21,640
 dentures are just too tall, and they're causing the patient to have to stay more open than

76
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:27,640
 they would be at rest, and so this is causing a lot of strain on the tissues and discomfort

77
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,680
 for the patient.

78
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:35,760
 Now how about the opposite problem, having an insufficient vertical dimension of occlusion?

79
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:42,000
 This would be where the intraclusal space is greater than 4 millimeters, and the patient

80
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:49,440
 has a long distance of closing between vertical dimension of rest and vertical dimension of

81
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:50,840
 occlusion.

82
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:55,040
 So for example, this could be a patient that just doesn't have any dentures and is missing

83
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:00,800
 all their teeth, so they have an extreme loss of vertical dimension of occlusion because

84
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,520
 they have no teeth to bite down on.

85
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:09,080
 So this can lead to a couple things, an aging appearance of the lower third of the face because

86
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:15,880
 of thin lips, wrinkles, the chin being too near to the nose, overlapping corners of the

87
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:21,240
 mouth, and all these things that would lead to someone looking a little bit older.

88
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:29,840
 A diminished occlusal force and angular colitis, which is that could be fungal infection, bacterial

89
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:36,920
 infection at the corner of the mouth, and so this is again due to this overlapping corners

90
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:43,140
 of the mouth with insufficient video, and so this folding in the mouth is just a perfect

91
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:48,640
 opportunity for an opportunistic infection like Canada to take over and cause angular

92
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:52,440
 colitis.

93
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:57,160
 Alright so we have all that information, now what can we do with it?

94
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:02,240
 So the CR record is something we talked about again in our previous video, so definitely

95
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:07,080
 check out those first two videos of the series if you haven't already, but for an agentulous

96
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:15,080
 patient the CR record provides the ability to increase or decrease the video more accurately

97
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:21,120
 in the articulator by establishing a radius of the mandibles arc of closure.

98
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:22,880
 Alright so what does that mean?

99
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:27,520
 Well in other words, if the video is incorrect like we just talked about in the previous

100
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:34,760
 two slides, you can change this by mounting the casts using a CR record, which remembers

101
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:41,320
 all about the patient's condyles, the MI record and the MI position is all about the

102
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:47,360
 teeth coming together, whereas the CR, the centric relation record, and that position

103
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:53,520
 is all about the condyles being at home in their fossa.

104
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:59,760
 So by mounting the casts using a CR record and then manipulating the guide pin on an

105
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:07,000
 articulator can lead you to changing the video and so you can do that in order to adjust

106
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,600
 if a patient is having an excessive video problem or an insufficient video problem.

107
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:19,440
 And this is just review from our second video, but the face bow again is critical for giving

108
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:26,480
 us information about where the maxilla is relative to the hinge axis that is the axis

109
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:30,520
 of rotation at the temporal mandibular joints.

110
00:09:30,520 --> 00:09:36,720
 So the CR record and face bow together again are those critical diagnostic pieces of information

111
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:46,840
 that we need to create and fabricate a proper set of dentures for that specific patient.

112
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:52,520
 Now we also have a certain record called the protrusive record, and this one registers

113
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:59,760
 the anterior, inferior, condyle path in the translation movement of the condyles.

114
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:05,280
 So in other words, when the patient moves the mandible forward, the condyles slide down

115
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:06,960
 their reticular eminences.

116
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:12,520
 This is that translation movement that I've talked about in my occlusion videos.

117
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:19,400
 And so the protrusive record is capturing this dynamic movement of the mandible forward

118
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,160
 and down.

119
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:26,920
 So we have this phenomenon, which is definitely going to be tested on the board exam, called

120
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:33,400
 the Christensen's Phenomenon, and it refers to the distal space created between the upper

121
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:39,520
 and lower occlusal surfaces when the mandible is protruded due to downward and forward movement

122
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:45,600
 of the condyles down their reticular eminences, like we talked about with the whole protrusive

123
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:46,800
 record.

124
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:54,040
 So another way to think about this is a posterior open bite is formed as the patient's mandible

125
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:57,120
 slides forward, like you can see in this image.

126
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:04,820
 Their separation or conclusion of the posterior teeth as the patient slides their jaw forward

127
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:10,800
 because the condyles don't just go straight forward, they come down and forward.

128
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:14,960
 And so that's opening up this posterior space.

129
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:21,520
 Now if you're talking about complete dentures, this could lead to possible instability.

130
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:26,680
 And so there's this thing called compensating curves that could be built into the denture

131
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:29,040
 to compensate for that.

132
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:32,720
 And so we're going to talk about that more a little bit later in this video.

133
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:38,160
 It's super cool to think about.

134
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,440
 And this is just a sneak peek of how the compensating curve would work.

135
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:47,880
 It's sort of instead of keeping the denture flat across like this horizontal line, you

136
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:54,000
 can curve the teeth back and up as you go posteriorly so that as the patient's sliding

137
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,320
 forward, all the teeth remain in contact.

138
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:03,520
 But again, I'll talk about that a little bit more in later slides.

139
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:10,640
 All right, so let's talk about the plane of occlusion and how we can measure that for

140
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:13,720
 a patient receiving dentures.

141
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:19,760
 So the camper's line is an imaginary line from the alla of the nose, that's the side

142
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:26,360
 of the nose to the tragus of the ear, which is that little part of skin that sticks out

143
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,120
 from your outer ear.

144
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:33,120
 And so if you draw an imaginary line, that would be your camper's line.

145
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:37,840
 And so if you remember alla of the nose, tragus of the ear, that would be something

146
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:45,840
 that again could be tested on the board exam as could the inter-pupillary line, which conveniently

147
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:49,640
 is just an imaginary line between the pupils of the eyes.

148
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:56,560
 So inter-pupillary, meaning between the eyes or the pupils of the eyes.

149
00:12:56,560 --> 00:13:03,280
 So the maxillary occlusal wax rim should be parallel to both of these lines.

150
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:08,440
 So maxillary occlusal wax rim, what's that talking about?

151
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:12,720
 Well I'm going to talk a little bit more about this in the next video, but the occlusal wax

152
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:18,120
 rim is what you would make after taking your impression.

153
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:24,080
 And what we would use to measure the maxillomandibular relations, that's the whole MMR thing that

154
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:28,180
 we talked about again in our introductory videos.

155
00:13:28,180 --> 00:13:34,640
 And you would measure the MMR and guide the lab technician where to place denture teeth

156
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:35,920
 in the trial denture.

157
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:41,120
 So this is really important, this is just a nice clipart picture of what a wax rim would

158
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:47,680
 be, patient would try it in, and we'd be able to see where that patient's camper's line

159
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:50,040
 inter-pupillary line would be.

160
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:55,240
 And we want to try to keep that maxillary wax rim parallel to both of those lines if

161
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:56,240
 possible.

162
00:13:56,240 --> 00:14:04,520
 And we can measure this with this metal instrument called a fox plane.

163
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:11,760
 Alright so balanced occlusion for complete dentures, it refers to simultaneous anterior

164
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:18,840
 and bilateral posterior contacts, a phenomenon known as tripodization in centric and eccentric

165
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:23,840
 movements to maintain seating of the dentures.

166
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:32,440
 So tripodization is an important concept, and it involves again contacts that are simultaneous

167
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:41,720
 in the front, so anterior contacts, and contacts on both the patient's right and left side.

168
00:14:41,720 --> 00:14:48,760
 When the patient's both biting down, that would be eccentric, and when they slide the

169
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:55,160
 mandible around, front, back, sideways, that is eccentric movements.

170
00:14:55,160 --> 00:15:00,880
 So we're getting this tripodization where all points of the denture, both front and both

171
00:15:00,880 --> 00:15:05,280
 sides of the back, are contacting all at once.

172
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:11,520
 So again that concept of the Christensen's phenomenon is ideally not occurring with dentures

173
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:17,040
 because we want to maintain contact as the patient slides around, so the dentures are

174
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:29,520
 being stable in place, and there's a constant force that's on them at all times.

175
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:35,860
 So that's what balanced occlusion is, talking about having balance between those all different

176
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:40,560
 points of the denture, so that one side of the denture is not getting more force than

177
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:45,040
 the other side, which could cause it to dislodge, the front's not getting more force than the

178
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:48,840
 back, which would cause it to dislodge, etc.

179
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:55,720
 So anterior guidance, however, should be avoided in complete denture occlusion to prevent

180
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:59,120
 dislodgement of the denture basis.

181
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:05,200
 So this is an important distinction here, because we like to have simultaneous anterior

182
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:12,600
 and bilateral posterior contacts in centric and eccentric, however, we don't want anterior

183
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:18,840
 guidance because this could cause dislodgement, this would be, we talked about it in one of

184
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:26,280
 the previous videos, anterior guidance referring to incisal guidance and canine guidance.

185
00:16:26,280 --> 00:16:34,720
 So anterior guidance is an umbrella term that includes both incisal and canine guidance.

186
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:40,920
 And this makes sense, right, because anterior guidance is focusing only on the incisors

187
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:46,920
 and the canines, and if those are the only teeth in contact, well, the posterior teeth

188
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:53,520
 would be excluded, and so now we have, we have an unbalanced occlusion on the dentures.

189
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:58,400
 There would be more contact on the anterior teeth than the posterior teeth, and we could

190
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:01,200
 have the dentures flip in dislodge.

191
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:08,760
 So we want to avoid that and instead have a nice balanced occlusion.

192
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:14,840
 So on the balancing side and on the working side, these are terms that are referred to

193
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:20,400
 in my dynamic occlusion video, so I will leave a link to that in the description where we

194
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,400
 talk all about this type of working and non-working movement.

195
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:28,560
 So if you'd like to learn more about that, definitely check out that video, I'll leave

196
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:31,160
 a link in the description.

197
00:17:31,160 --> 00:17:36,080
 All right, so we had this balanced occlusion concept.

198
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:41,200
 Now let's talk about lingualized occlusion, which is another sort of occlusal scheme.

199
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:47,200
 These are all occlusal schemes used in denture fabrication and tooth setting and tooth position,

200
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:51,360
 and so lingualized occlusion is another one just to be familiar with.

201
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:58,520
 So it's where only the palatal cusps of the maxillary posterior teeth contact the mandibri

202
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:04,640
 posterior posterior teeth, theoretically eliminating destabilizing buckle for specters.

203
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:09,040
 So you probably don't have to know too much about the details of this.

204
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:13,120
 There's all this neutral centric occlusion and all that.

205
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:18,560
 I think the only thing you would need to know for the board exam with lingualized occlusion

206
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:25,000
 is that it's all about here's the upper teeth and the lower teeth, and this is from sort

207
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,880
 of a coronal view.

208
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:34,120
 This would be the palatal cusp of the upper posterior teeth and it's contacting in the

209
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:41,560
 central fossa or marginal ridges of the lower posterior teeth, and that is the only contact

210
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:46,520
 that we want in lingualized occlusion.

211
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:51,800
 So the lingual cusp of upper teeth into the lower fossas and marginal ridges being the

212
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:58,960
 only point of contact which could create a very simple and stable force vector to avoid

213
00:18:58,960 --> 00:18:59,960
 again.

214
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:05,160
 We're just trying to avoid dislodging of the dentures during function.

215
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:11,000
 So we have balanced occlusion which is all about tripotization and we have lingualized

216
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:17,240
 occlusion which is all about upper palatal cusps being the only point of contact.

217
00:19:17,240 --> 00:19:22,800
 And that's really the gist of it, that's the most important stuff I would take away from

218
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:26,320
 balanced and lingualized occlusion.

219
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:34,200
 All right, so before we end the video I'll talk about these Bennett concepts, there's

